An `effect` inside a component the build cannot inline belongs to the instance,
so everything it needs is declared inside `__cf_Row` and settles from there:
`__effects0_settle()` is called once, at the end of the factory, after the node
the mount handler writes to exists.

The page has two scopes and one of them is not the factory's. Five of the html
emitters walked pkg.Components and decided for themselves whether a component's
declarations were theirs to write, and three of them never asked. bodyCalls
collected this component's settle entry point as if it were the page's, so the
startup script called `__effects0_settle()` at module scope -- and the only
definition, inside the factory, had been renamed `__effects0_settle2` by the
collision that page-scope declaration itself caused. The teardown the page
registered on `pagehide` was the same name in the same way.

	the page raised on load:
	"Uncaught ReferenceError: __effects0_settle is not defined"

pageComponents is the one list all of them read now, so the indentation below
is the assertion: every effect name is inside the factory, and nothing about
this effect appears at page scope.

deny out/none/html/index.html `(?m)^  __effects0_settle` -- an instance's effect settles from inside its factory; the page has no instance to settle
deny out/none/html/index.html `(?m)^  window.addEventListener\("pagehide"` -- the page registers no teardown for an effect it does not own; the instance's __destroy runs it
deny out/none/html/index.html `settle2|__snglTeardown2` -- a name suffixed by the emitter is a collision, and the collision is a declaration the page should not have made

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component Row(label string) node {
    var log list<string> = []
    effect(on=label, @mount { log.push("+") }, @unmount { log.push("-") })
    text(value=label + "[" + log.join(",") + "]")
}

component App() node {
    var on = true
    button(text="toggle", @click { on = !on })
    if on {
        Row(label="x")
    }
}

window(title="H") {
    App()
}
